--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/models/glm-5.3-flash/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,158 @@
+# GLM-5.3-Flash (`glm5-next`)
+
+The HIP engine supports the GLM-5.3-Flash autoregressive trunk, including
+mHC, KDA recurrence, sparse latent MLA/indexing and sigmoid-routed MoE.
+The serving adapter uses the shared request scheduler and TP2 instruction
+protocol, with the artifact's `glm4` tokenizer and pinned compiled template.
+
+## Qualified serving configuration
+
+- SixVolts `GLM-5.3-Flash-Q4_K_XL`, five GGUF shards.
+- Two `gfx1151` hosts, TP2 over tbstream, **F32 wire** on both ranks.
+- **One active session**, 4096-token allocated context, prefill chunks up
+  to 4096 tokens (the engine's `kMaxBatch`; **4096 is the qualified
+  default** after the weights-stationary pair re-sweep), autoregressive
+  decoding.
+- Chat Completions and Responses, buffered and streaming; raw Completions.
+- Native GLM tools: auto, required, named, zero-argument, nested JSON,
+  literal XML/Unicode and parallel calls, with tool-result replay.
+- EOS, stop strings, output limits, invalid-request recovery, prefill and
+  decode cancellation followed immediately by another request.
+
+An omitted `--context` selects 4096 for GLM. The runtime capacity ceiling
+is 262,144 tokens; the actual 32K/128K/256K context ladder is a separate,
+pending qualification. The native artifact's 1M limit is not a serving claim.
+
+The trunk's resident weights are approximately 85.91 GiB per TP rank.
+The first-leg engine has approximately 756 MiB of shared scratch and
+166 MiB of session state at 4096 tokens. Measured total device usage in
+the serving qualification was approximately 89.1 GiB per rank. The loader
+checks the planned weight share against available device memory before
+uploading it. The full TP1 checkpoint does not fit a 120 GiB device budget.
+
+## Starting the pair
+
+Build the transport-enabled package with `nix build .#tp2-tbstream` and
+use the same artifact on both ranks. After the documented tbstream bring-up,
+rank 0 runs:
+
+```sh
+GUFO_TBSTREAM_WIRE=f32 gufo serve llm \
+  --model /path/to/GLM-5.3-Flash-Q4_K_XL-00001-of-00005.gguf \
+  --served-model-name glm-5.3-flash \
+  --tp-world-size 2 --tp-rank 0 --tp-transport tbstream \
+  --tp-control-token "$TP_CONTROL_TOKEN" \
+  --context 4096 --sessions 1 --prefill-chunk 4096 \
+  --speculative off --think on --reasoning-effort low
+```
+
+Rank 1 uses the same options with `--tp-rank 1` and
+`--tp-bootstrap-host "$RANK0_HOST"`. Rank 1 executes mirrored work and
+serves no HTTP traffic. Both ranks must agree on context, chunk geometry,
+vocabulary and grammar semantics. Serving rejects a missing reducer/control
+channel or a model partition that differs from the serving TP configuration.
+
+## Reasoning and tools
+
+The native template always opens `<think>`. Reasoning is split from content
+in both APIs; explicit thinking-off requests are rejected. Native effort
+defaults to `max`: provider-neutral minimal/low maps to low, medium/high
+to high, and xhigh/max to max. Previous reasoning follows the template's
+preserve-by-default behavior; explicit discard maps to `clear_thinking`.
+
+The sampling preset uses temperature 1.0 and top-p 0.95, matching the
+official BF16 model card's tool-assisted evaluation settings. Explicit
+server/request sampling controls override the preset.
+
+Native tool calls have this form:
+
+```text
+<tool_call>function_name<arg_key>key</arg_key><arg_value>value</arg_value></tool_call>
+```
+
+String argument whitespace is preserved. Non-string arguments use JSON
+values; declared schemas guide type recovery. Native constraints add no
+second tool protocol or prompt instruction. The TP peer rebuilds the same
+constraint from its recipe, and the parser distinguishes GLM from Qwen
+despite their shared `<tool_call>` opener.
+
+## Continuation and cancellation
+
+The engine retains one mutable executed frontier. Exact token-prefix
+extensions reuse it; divergent or truncated histories reset and replay.
+Immutable snapshots and disk persistence are not advertised by this adapter.
+Real client histories can change tokens when reasoning/calls are re-rendered;
+cache metrics expose the actual reused and recomputed work.
+
+Cancellation is coordinated between mirrored work units. An already-running
+chunk completes before cancellation takes effect. In the qualification, an
+immediate request after cancelling a 350-token prefill recovered in about
+47 seconds, while the pair remained ready. GLM uses a bounded 180-second
+worker-admission window because its eager work units can exceed the shared
+30-second default.
+
+## Throughput (measured)
+
+Serving-level prefill on the qualified two-host TP2 pair (tbstream, f32
+wire, context 32768, **chunk 4096, the re-qualified width after the
+weights-stationary kernel campaign**): **115.0 tok/s** for ~3.4K-token
+cold prompts (fresh tokens / server `prefill_ms`, three calibrated runs
+114.56-115.58, every run `cached_tokens = 0`; client-side wall agrees
+within 0.02 tok/s; the served width is proven per request -
+`max_prefill_chunk_tokens == min(fresh, 4096)`). Comparison points at
+matched depths: 105.9 tok/s at ~5.9K tokens and 86.7 tok/s at ~15.9K
+(the depth-dependent MLA/indexer families; a 32K LCG probe measured
+62.0 tok/s pre-split). The weights-stationary campaign moved serving
+prefill 84.4 -> 110.3 tok/s (+30.6 %; the pre-campaign package served
+75.0) and the split-launch sparse MLA of the closing phase took it to
+**115.0** (+4.3 %; the MLA attention family itself -34 % per chunk).
+Method: `tests/functional/glm53_pair.py` brings the pair up;
+`glm53_serving_prefill.py` calibrates the filler-block token rate with
+two sends, then measures streamed requests with `return_progress`,
+asserting `cached_tokens < 200` (measured 0 on every qualifying run; the
+server log independently shows `cache=miss cached_tokens=0`) so every
+run prefills a cold prefix. The LCG probe (`glm5_next_tp_probe`) agrees
+within ~2.5 % (112.7 tok/s at 4096-token depth, chunk 4096). Full record
+with the ranked residual-bottleneck analysis against the >= 250 tok/s
+objective: `notes/final-ws-250.md` in the goal worktree.
+
+Where the number comes from (2026-10-10/11 campaigns, goals
+`2026-10-10-glm-prefill-250` and `-vllm`): serving prefill moved 35.1 ->
+75.0 -> 84.4 tok/s (expert grouped-GEMM pair tiling, 16-token quantized
+projection tiles, multi-query tiled sparse MLA, 1024-wide serving chunks,
+register-column KDA delta-rule scan) and then 84.4 -> 110.3 tok/s via the
+weight-stationary rewrites of the three mover families (expert
+grouped-GEMM weight-stationary tiles, Q8_0 re-sweep, F32 tiled float
+accumulation, 4096-wide serving chunks; 6.56x on the F32 family alone),
+and 110.3 -> 115.0 via the score/gather split-launch sparse MLA with
+cooperative softmax exponentials - bit-identical outputs throughout (the
+chunked/tokenwise byte identity, CPU-oracle and cross-rank gates hold at
+every step; the split's pair-oracle per-token lines reproduce the
+pre-split tree's byte-for-byte). A measured roofline closes further gains
+within the correctness contracts: the expert family is
+register/occupancy-bound (phase 2) and the Q8_0 family streams its
+actual traffic at ~99 % of the 240 GB/s DRAM ceiling (phase 3), so the
+remaining 2.17x to 250 tok/s sits in execution-model changes, the
+indexer double-precision contract and the serial stack - see
+`notes/final-ws-250.md` and `notes/final-250.md` in the goal worktrees
+and the 250-campaign appendendum in
+`/root/strix-dual-exp/evidence/glm53-flash/p5/OPTIMIZATION.md`.
+
+Decode on the pair is exchange-latency-bound (~140 ms/token at 90
+lockstep exchanges per step, full model); the single-host decode guard
+is ~16.7 ms/token.
+
+## Qualification and follow-up
+
+The P4 functional driver is `tests/functional/glm53_tp2.py`; it attaches to an
+already-running, isolated pair and retains requests, raw HTTP/SSE responses,
+individual timings and verdicts. Supply `--server-log` to correlate Responses
+queue/TTFT timings with rank 0's request IDs.
+
+P4 passed all 18 driver cases. A real OpenCode agent completed a bounded
+read/edit/Python-test/report task through filesystem/test MCP tools, with
+independent verification of the changed source. A two-session Qwen TP2
+control passed all nine constraint cases on the same candidate package.
+
+Follow-up: real-history continuation characterization, the 4K→32K→128K→256K
+recall/cancellation/memory ladder, then the measured autoregressive baseline.
