index 00000000..232a6212
--- /dev/null
+++ b/notes/baseline-250.md
@@ -0,0 +1,163 @@
+# GLM-5.3-Flash TP2 prefill 250 tok/s — phase 1 baseline (2026-10-10)
+
+Cold-start baseline for the goal `2026-10-10-glm-prefill-250`: reproduce the
+slice bench, re-run every slice gate on the current tree, and refresh the
+kernel profile so later phases argue from today's numbers, not P5's.
+No functional source changes in this phase (see "Tree hygiene" for the two
+exceptions that are reformat-only).
+
+## Carried-forward baselines (2026-10-10 P5 session)
+
+Source: `/root/strix-dual-exp/evidence/glm53-flash/p5/OPTIMIZATION.md`.
+
+| Measurement | P5 result |
+| --- | ---: |
+| 4-layer slice prefill, TP1 dev bench (`glm5_next_prefill_bench`, 4096 tok, chunk 512) | 253 tok/s |
+| Pair probe prefill, TP2, 2048 LCG tokens | 37.1 tok/s |
+| Serving prefill, TP2 HTTP, 3426-token prompt | 35.1 tok/s |
+
+Today's reproduction (same binary defaults, `gpu-test` build, worktree at
+goal base `431f7a1`):
+
+```
+prefill pass 0: 4096 tokens in 16066.0 ms (254.9 tok/s)
+prefill pass 1: 4096 tokens in 16112.4 ms (254.2 tok/s)
+BEST 254.9 tok/s (layers 4, chunk 512)
+```
+
+Confirmed: the ~253 tok/s slice baseline reproduces (254.9 tok/s, +0.7%).
+The gap to close is the pair/serving path: 37.1 → 250 tok/s ≈ 6.7×.
+
+## Correctness gates (all on `build/gpu-test`, this worktree)
+
+Model `/root/models/glm53-flash/GLM-5.3-Flash-Q4_K_XL-00001-of-00005.gguf`,
+fixtures `/root/strix-dual-exp/evidence/glm53-flash/p2/fixtures`.
+
+| Gate | Result | Detail |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `glm5_next_mhc_ops_test` | **PASS** | worst mhc \|d\| 2.4e-7, block-in 2.6e-7, collapsed 2.0e-7, logits 1.6e-7 (top-1 matched) |
+| `glm5_next_kda_ops_test` | **PASS** | worst kda rel rms 2.3e-7, block out 2.8e-7, logits 1.6e-7 |
+| `glm5_next_dsa_ops_test` | **PASS** | full four-layer slice on real weights |
+| `glm5_next_executor_test` (5-token oracle) | **PASS** | worst logits rel_rms 7.6e-7, top-1 matched, reset replay exact |
+| `glm5_next_executor_test --random-tokens 513 1 --skip-oracle` | **PASS** | logits + persistent state byte-identical, 2.93 ms/token batched |
+| `glm5_next_executor_test --random-tokens 2052 1 --skip-oracle` | **PASS** | logits + persistent state byte-identical, 3.51 ms/token batched |
+| `glm5_next_batch_select_test` | **PASS** | tiled batch pool selector contract |
+| `tools/ci/check-format.py` | **PASS** | after `--fix` of two pre-existing violations (see below) |
+
+CPU-oracle RMS caps (5e-5 projections / 2e-4 logits), chunk-vs-tokenwise byte
+identity, and the double-precision indexer top-k (still exact-double per P5)
+all hold on this tree.
+
+## Slice profile (rocprofv3 via `tools/prof/prof.py run --stages glm`)
+
+Trace covers both bench passes: 2 × 8 chunks of 512 = 16 chunk executions,
+8192 tokens total, 2937 dispatches, kernel-time sum 32244.6 ms, GPU busy
+95.1% of the 33.9 s span (4.9% idle — the slice path is GPU-bound, not
+launch-bound, today). Per-token = total / 8192; per-chunk = total / 16.
+
+| # | Kernel | % of kernel time | per-token ms | per-512-chunk ms | calls/chunk |
+| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| 1 | `BatchQuantTiledKernel` (Q8_0 projections) | 36.9% | 1.454 | 744.6 | 27 |
+| 2 | `BatchMlaAttentionKernel` (DSA sparse attention) | 31.4% | 1.237 | 633.7 | 1 |
+| 3 | `BatchExpertGroupedKernel` (MoE experts, Q4_K) | 12.1% | 0.474 | 242.9 | 3 |
+| 4 | `BatchF32TiledKernel` (F32 projections) | 8.3% | 0.327 | 167.6 | 29 |
+| 5 | `BatchKdaRecurrenceKernel` (KDA delta-rule scan) | 5.6% | 0.220 | 112.5 | 3 |
+
+Top-5 = 94.3% of kernel time. Next: MLA absorb 1.4%, indexer scores 1.3%,
+RmsNorm 1.1%. The 4-layer slice is layers 0–2 KDA (dense FFN 12288) + layer 3
+DSA (first MoE block; 288 experts × 8 active, expert FFN 2048, Q4_K).
+
+## Roofline estimate per family (TP1 slice, 512-token chunk)
+
+DRAM ceiling taken as 240 GB/s (the number this repo's own gfx1151 bench
+comments use; achieved fractions below are computed from measured kernel
+time and analytic traffic). Weight-table sizes from the P0 inventory dims.
+
+| Family | Weight table / working set per chunk | Traffic today | Implied BW | Re-read factor vs weights-once | Stationary floor |
+| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| `BatchQuantTiledKernel` | ~0.94 GB (KDA q/k/v 9×35.7 MB; dense gate/up/down 9×53.5 MB; DSA q_a/q_b/out 105 MB; indexer ~30 MB) | ~121 GB | ~163 GB/s (68% of ceiling) | **~128×** | ~2 GB ⇒ ~8–12 ms |
+| `BatchMlaAttentionKernel` | F16 latent cache 8 MiB @4096 depth + per-head k_b/v_b F32 ~50 MB | <0.5 GB | ~0.4–0.8 GB/s | ~1× (cache resident) | not DRAM-bound; ~O(100 GFLOP) ⇒ ~4 ms at fp32 peak — measured 634 ms is ~1–2% of either roofline ⇒ **serialization/latency-bound** |
+| `BatchExpertGroupedKernel` | 4.08 GB (288 experts × 3 × 4096×2048 × 0.5625 B), one MoE layer in slice | ~14.7 GB | ~61 GB/s (25% of ceiling) | **~3.6×** (⌈14.2 pairs / 4-pair tile⌉) | 4.08 GB ⇒ 17 ms @240 (68 ms @61) |
+| `BatchF32TiledKernel` | ~0.15–0.25 GB (kv_a, k_b/v_b, indexer proj, hc fn, ssm f/g/beta) | ~19–32 GB | ~115–190 GB/s (48–79%; P5 measured this family within ~2× of its traffic bound, favouring the high end) | **~128×** | ~0.25 GB ⇒ ~1–1.5 ms |
+| `BatchKdaRecurrenceKernel` | state 4 MB + ~0.05 GB traffic | ~0.05 GB | <1 GB/s | n/a | 0.2 ms BW floor ⇒ **serial-scan latency-bound** |
+
+Weight-traffic arithmetic: `kTileTokens = 4`
+(`src/models/glm5_next/kernels/rocm/kernels_batch.hip.cpp:256`), so every
+weight table is re-read once per 4-token tile — 512/4 = 128 times per
+512-token chunk. The profiled grid confirms it (`blocks=512x128x1`, y = 128
+token tiles).
+
+## Thesis check
+
+- **Weight-stationary M-scaling is the dominant lever.** The three
+  weight-moving families (quant tiled + expert grouped + F32 tiled) cost
+  744.6 + 242.9 + 167.6 = 1155 ms/chunk = 2.25 ms/token, i.e. 57% of the
+  3.94 ms/token slice total. Their weights-once-per-chunk floor is ~5.3 GB
+  ⇒ ~22 ms/chunk at the 240 GB/s ceiling (~31 ms at today's achieved BW) —
+  a ~30× traffic reduction on those families. Everything else equal, that
+  alone moves the slice from ~3.9 to ~1.7 ms/token (~580 tok/s ceiling) and
+  applies identically to the pair's half-rank tables. In the P5 *pair*
+  profile the same families were expert ~40% + quant ~18% + F32 ~13% =
+  71% of pair prefill time, so the lever is even more dominant there.
+  N is 4 today because the LDS staging (`staged[kTileTokens][kTileK]`,
+  stride-33 padded) prices T linearly in shared memory; raising N means
+  restructuring staging (e.g. split-K staging or register-resident x) so
+  weight rows amortize over more tokens.
+- **Expert buckets average ~14 tokens/expert at 512-token chunks**,
+  confirmed analytically: 512 tokens × 8 active experts / 288 experts =
+  14.2 pairs/expert — matching P5's measured ~14. With 4 pairs per tile the
+  expert table is re-read ~3.6× per chunk; bucket-parallel or T-scaled
+  expert tiles are the second-half of the same stationarity lever.
+- **MLA attention is the next wall, and it is not a bandwidth problem.**
+  633.7 ms per chunk at <1 GB/s effective DRAM and ~1–2% of flop peak means
+  serialization (per-block key scans); the fix direction is occupancy/ILP,
+  not traffic. In the slice bench this family is inflated by TP1 (64 heads)
+  and full 4096 depth; TP2 serving halves the heads.
+- **KDA recurrence (0.220 ms/token)** is a serial 512-step scan; lever is
+  scan parallelism (chunked/blocked scan), independent of stationarity.
+- After M-scaling, the 4.9% idle fraction and the KDA scan become the
+  likely next bottlenecks at slice level.
+
+## Production and environment
+
+- Production (gufo-prod, port 15003) ACTIVE throughout; `/ready` = **200**
+  checked before the gates and re-checked after the profile (phase-end
+  check below). Slice tests coexisted with it as expected; no full-model
+  GPU tests were run, nothing was parked.
+- Worktree cold-start fix: the goal worktree is a shallow git clone, so
