+## Evidence chain for the accumulation switch (the brief's a-d)
+
+**(a) FLOP-bound on f64 throughput, measured.**
+- On-chip FMA ceilings from the bench (chains of 8, 640x256 threads):
+  **f64 461.5 GFLOP/s, f32 8385.3 GFLOP/s** — 18.2:1 (RDNA3.5's cracked
+  FP64 pipe).
+- Family FLOP demand 1.181 TFLOP / 2048 tokens ⇒ **a perfect double kernel
+  (100% f64 issue, zero traffic/latency overhead) floors at 2560 ms —
+  above the 2200 ms mandate**. The landed double kernel measured 6531 ms
+  = 180 GFLOP/s = 39% of the f64 ceiling while at only 15% of the DRAM
+  read ceiling (37 GB/s): the only resource it pushes toward saturation is
+  the FP64 pipe.
+- **Traffic control**: weight-stationary DOUBLE variants cutting the weight
+  re-read 2-4x (D16/D16R2/D32/D32R2 — T=16/32 tiles, LDS [T][512] = 32/64
+  KiB, all bit-identical twins of V0, verified) measured **0.97-1.02x of
+  V0** at every width (family model: 7118 → 6996-7315 ms/2048 standalone).
+  Under double accumulation the traffic term is not binding at any swept
+  tile, i.e. the family is f64-issue-bound "at weights-once traffic" in the
+  only sense reachable with ≤64 accumulator doubles per lane (the true
+  weights-once T=chunk tile needs 1024 accumulators; registers cap sums at
+  ~64 doubles/lane — the D32R2 corner, 149 VGPRs, already shows it).
+- ISA mix (`hipcc --cuda-device-only -S` + `tools/prof/isa_mix.py`,
+  /tmp/opencode/f32ws/f32_ws_bench.s): V0's inner loop is dominated by
+  64-bit VALU arithmetic — per unrolled body v_add_f64 x40 + v_fma_f64 x8 +
+  v_cvt_f64_f32 x9 against 8 LDS reads; the float kernel's mix moves to
+  fp32 FMAs.
+- rocprofv3 HW pipe counters were attempted (pmc TXT, YAML and JSON input
+  forms, `-i` with `-f csv`): the tool executes the workload but emits no
+  counter database on this stack (rocm 7.2.3; kernel-trace mode — what
+  tools/prof/prof.py uses — works fine). The f64-bound conclusion rests on
+  the peak microbench + FLOP arithmetic + traffic controls above, which
+  bound the utilization question without pipe counters.
+
+**(b) ≥1.5x family speedup in the standalone prototype**: the winner
+measured **9.82x** on the calls-weighted family model (below); integrated
+6.56x on the pair (in-situ, TP2, profiled rank0).
+
+**(c) oracle re-run**: `glm5_next_executor_test` 5-token CPU oracle PASS —
+worst logits rel_rms **7.484e-07 (double) → 7.481e-07 (float)**, cap 2e-4
+unchanged, no re-derivation needed (the float projection error is invisible
+under the quantization-vs-CPU term). Direct per-projection numerics (bench,
+float vs double outputs, real shapes, k=4096/1536/128): max|diff| ≤ 2.4e-07,
+rel_rms ≤ **5.9e-08**; k=128 shapes 5.0e-08.
+
+**(d) both twins switched together**: the tokenwise path
+(`F32Gemm` → `BatchF32Gemm(n=1)`) and the batched path launch the same two
+kernels; per-(row, token) statement order, lane-run assignment and the
+single butterfly are the landed kernel's, and the tile width T is a
+property of the kernel, not the invocation — so chunked-vs-tokenwise byte
+identity holds by construction and was re-verified byte-exact at 513 x3 /
+2052 / 2049 (exec gates below), plus the bench's twin pattern
+A(n=N) == A(n=1 x N) and cross-template xdispatch checks (140+ checks, 0
+diffs). **The indexer top-k is NOT relaxed**: the selection arithmetic
+(`BatchIndexerScoresKernel` double dot products, serial double softmax
+denominator, double-precision `BatchSelectPoolsKernel` scoring) is
+untouched — verified by source inspection this phase; only the projection
+GEMMs feeding it changed, under the CPU-oracle caps.
