+## Roofline: why ≥ 250 tok/s is not reachable within this goal's contracts
+
+Machine ceilings, measured today with the repo's own
+`tools/bench/gfx1151_peak.hip`: **DRAM read 240.12 GB/s**, DRAM write
+216.33, DRAM copy 209.89, LDS 6724.79. The TB link is not the binding
+constraint (wire census ≈ 0.78 ms/token of write that overlaps stage;
+write+wait ≈ 5% of prefill wall).
+
+**Bytes-per-token lower bound (per rank, from the P0 inventory dims).**
+Per-rank weight tables: expert Q4_K 43.8 GiB (43 MoE layers × 144
+experts × 3 × 1024×4096 elts × 0.5625 B), Q8_0 dense projections ≈ 3.81
+GiB (KDA q/k/v/out 2.42 + DSA 0.60 + dense FFN 0.23 + shared experts
+0.56), F32 tables ≈ 0.89 GiB (k_b/v_b 0.36, router 0.19, ssm f/g 0.13,
+kv_a 0.09, hc.fn 0.07, indexer 0.05); embeddings+head 1.28 GiB are read
+effectively once per chunk. The landed tile shapes re-read tables by
+construction: Q8 and expert tiles are 16 tokens/pairs wide, F32 tiles 8
+tokens. Per 1024-token chunk that mandates:
+
+| family | tile re-read | traffic per chunk | measured time | achieved BW | fraction of read ceiling |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| expert Q4_K grouped | ceil(28.4/16)=2 | 87.6 GiB = 94.1 GB | 3479 ms | 27 GB/s | 11% |
+| Q8_0 quant tiled | 1024/16 = 64 | 244 GiB = 262 GB | 2150 ms | 122 GB/s | 51% |
+| F32 tiled (double acc.) | 1024/8 = 128 | 114 GiB = 122 GB | 3271 ms | 37 GB/s | 15% |
+| **total weight stream** | | **≈ 479 GB** | | | |
+
+Serial/latency-bound families (attention gathers, norms, indexer scoring,
+HC Sinkhorn, KDA scan/prepare, activations): measured **2.27 ms/token**
+after this phase's KDA win (MLA attention alone is 0.81 ms/token after
+phase 4's 5–6× tiling; it is gather/serialization-bound, not DRAM-bound).
+
+**The bound.** 250 tok/s is a 4.00 ms/token budget. The mandated weight
+stream alone is 479 GB per 1024 tokens = **1.95 ms/token at 100% of the
+measured 240.12 GB/s pure-read ceiling** (itself an unattainable
+idealization for mixed-stream GEMM kernels; the mixed-traffic ceiling
+measures 210–216). Adding only the measured serial families (2.27
+ms/token — which cannot stream from DRAM at all) gives
+**4.22 ms/token > 4.00 even in the idealized case**: the weight movers
+would need 270 GB/s, 12% above the machine's measured peak read
+bandwidth. Equivalently, holding the best measured family efficiency
+(51%, the Q8 tile) across all three movers leaves them at 3.8 ms/token
+before anything else runs. **Therefore ≥ 250 tok/s serving-level prefill
+is unreachable on 2× Strix Halo within this goal's constraint set** —
+tile shapes are bounded by the 64 KiB LDS cap (T=32 exceeds it for both
+the quant and expert kernels; phases 2–4 swept these axes to their
+corners), F32 accumulation must stay double (CPU-oracle contract), and
+the KDA scan must stay exact-order (byte-identity contract). Best
+achieved: **84.4 tok/s serving / 89.2 tok/s probe / 22457 ms pair kernel
+time**.
+
+Escape routes that would change the bound (all outside this goal's
+contracts, listed for the next campaign): (a) persistent
+weight-stationary kernel re-architecture approaching weights-once
+traffic (86.9 GiB per chunk → 0.36 ms/token at ceiling; a ground-up
+kernel program, not a tile retune); (b) float accumulation in the F32
+family (a numerics-contract change: the 5e-5 projection cap would have
+to be re-derived against the CPU oracle); (c) further MLA/indexer
+latency work (2.27 ms/token of serial cost). Note (a) is the only one
+that moves the bytes-per-token lower bound itself.
+
