+| `tools/ci/check-format.py` | **PASS** | (the `.hip` bench file is outside its suffix filter, styled manually per phase-2 precedent) |
+
+## Dispatch documentation (the brief's ask)
+
+One canonical path: `BatchQ8KBlockGEMV` → `BatchQuantTiledKernel` for every
+Q8_0/sub16 dense projection at every n (including the 0.67 GB n=1 vocabulary
+head, 0.127 ms/launch), with the m%64≠0 wave-GEMV fallback unchanged. The
+small-n sweep (n ∈ {1, 8, 16, 32, 64} × 7 shapes) shows V0 fastest at every
+point — no regime boundary to draw, no new dispatch.
+
+## Environment / provenance
+
+- Prototype: `tools/bench/quant_ws_bench.hip` (+ `-ffp-contract=on` for this
+  bench in `tools/bench/build.sh`); runs `/tmp/opencode/quantws/bench*.log`,
+  resource report `/tmp/quant_ws_bench.res.txt`.
+- Fresh slice profile: `/tmp/opencode/quantws/prof/prof_results.db`
+  (rocprofv3, `--stages glm`, 8192 tokens / 512-chunks, 2937 dispatches).
+- Production stayed up throughout; `/ready` = 200 checked before the gates
+  and re-checked at phase end. No pair probe (above).
+- One stack defect recorded for the next phases: global-x kernels with
+  ScratchSize > 0 fault (illegal address) under back-to-back concurrent
+  launches on rocm 7.2.3. Does not affect the landed kernel (0 scratch).
+
+## Next-phase pointers
+
+- The family's remaining lever is the **x-restaging audience**, an
+  executor/dataflow change: gate+up sharing one staged tile audience
+  (the traffic model's x term halves per fused audience only if the ROW
+  BLOCKS share staging — fusing the launches alone does nothing, checked:
+  block count and staging count are both (Σm)/rpb), K-split staging with a
+  deterministic combine (contract analysis needed), or rpb > 64 via
+  warp-specialized staging. None are tile-geometry changes; all need the
+  byte-identity analysis this phase's harness now automates.
+- The F32 tile (21.4% of this phase's fresh slice profile) is FP64-bound by
+  contract — a numerics relaxation case, not a tiling case.
+- The serial term (2.27 ms/token) and the MLA/KDA families dominate the
+  post-mover budget; the Q8 family is done — at the mixed-traffic ceiling.
